            "<b>Without.</b> See the Be'er Heitev and see in Beit David who wrote that if one remembered at the end of the day during twilight and counted then without a blessing he should count the remaining days without a blessing as there is no complete double doubt, and in Be'er Yitzchak he wavered somewhat on this, and see in Eshel Avraham regarding the law of an onen, and in Noda BiYehuda siman 27 he wrote that if the cemetery was far away and one will be an onen for a day and a night he should count at night without a blessing and the remaining days with a blessing, and in Be'er Yitzchak he brought in the name of Pri HaAretz manuscript that he counts the remaining days without a blessing, and in Machzik Bracha he wrote that nevertheless if the onen counted on his own and of his own will and there was someone attending to the burial needs on his behalf then for the remaining days he may recite a blessing, and see there in Be'er Yitzchak in the name of Pri HaAretz that a minor who came of age during the days of the Omer and similarly a convert who converted during the days of the Omer count the remaining days without a blessing and if he converted on the first day of the Omer he counts that same day without a blessing and afterwards with a blessing, and what he wrote regarding a minor does not apply according to our practice since minors who reached the age of chinuch also count and all the more so one approaching adulthood and if he did not count until he came of age and the days of the Omer had already passed it is obvious he does not recite a blessing, and see in Machzik Bracha in the name of Zera Emet that one who forgot to count on Friday night and they prayed the Shabbat evening service while it was still day and then remembered he had not counted may still count without a blessing and the remaining days with a blessing, and see in Machzik Bracha regarding one who forgot and did not count one night and the following night was the prayer leader and fulfilled the obligation for others through his blessing, where Sama D'Chayei concluded that even if the others had not counted for themselves and fulfilled their obligation through this prayer leader they may still count the remaining days with a blessing, and regarding whether one who forgot and counts without a blessing can serve as prayer leader to fulfill the obligation for others the Pri Chadash wrote that he cannot and in Mikra'ei Kodesh he disagreed and in Be'er Yitzchak he brought in the name of the responsa of Kneset HaGedola and Sama D'Chayei and Beit David in accordance with the Pri Chadash and concluded and so is our ruling."
